course: dictionary
lesson: 15464
title: One word, four meanings: stocks
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word «stocks» has more than one meaning, so the words around it tell you which one is right. Check the sentence, swap your meaning in, and see if it still makes sense. «The shop stocks toys» is not about a wooden frame: here «stocks» is a verb, and it means the shop keeps toys to sell.
explain: «Stocks» can mean supplies kept ready to use: «We have good stocks of food for the winter.» It can also mean shares, small parts of a company that people buy. «Dad bought stocks in a toy company.» The clue is «bought» and «company», so this meaning is about money.
explain: Long ago, «the stocks» were a wooden frame with holes for the feet, and sometimes the hands. «In the Middle Ages, a thief was put in the stocks in the village square.» The clue is «put» and «village square»: people were fixed in the stocks as a punishment in public, so this is a history meaning.
fact: «Stocks» is the plural of the noun «stock». «Stocks of food» means many supplies kept ready to use.
fact: «Stock» is the singular form, as in «a stock of paper», meaning one supply kept ready to use.
fact: «Stocks» can mean shares in a company, which people buy so they own a small part of it.
fact: «In the stocks» means fixed in a wooden frame with holes for the feet, a punishment used long ago in public.
fact: «Stocks» is also the he or she form of the verb «stock»: «The shop stocks toys» means it keeps toys to sell. Phrases like «in stock» and «out of stock» are about supplies in a shop.
exercise: «The shop stocks toys.» What does stocks mean in this sentence? | It is a bag for carrying toys | It is a share in a toy company | It is a wooden frame for punishing people | *It keeps toys to sell
exercise: «Dad bought stocks in a big company.» What are stocks in this sentence? | Supplies of food kept ready to use | Wooden frames for the feet | *Shares, small parts of a company that people buy
exercise: «Our cupboard has good stocks of pasta for emergencies.» What does stocks mean here? | Shares that people buy in a company | *Supplies kept ready to use | Long ago punishments in the village square
test: «In the Middle Ages, villagers were put in the stocks.» What were the stocks? | A kind of shop that sells toys | Supplies of food kept for winter | *A wooden frame with holes for the feet, used to punish people in public | A type of boat
test: Mia says, «The shelves are empty, and the stocks are running low.» What does Mia mean? | The company is making lots of money | *The supplies are nearly all used up | The wooden frame is ready for use | The shop has too many things to sell
exam: In «The bakery stocks fresh bread every morning», what does stocks mean? | It is a supply of flour kept for emergencies | It is a wooden frame with holes for the feet | It is a share in a bakery company | *It keeps fresh bread to sell
